issues.body:
Follow-up to [GHSA-vh66-26gq-q6x8](https://github.com/axios/axios/security/advisories/GHSA-vh66-26gq-q6x8). I can still reproduce inherited-header replacement with the published Axios **1.20.0** fetch adapter.

This is the advisory's existing read-side gadget after **prior same-process prototype pollution**, not a claim that Axios supplies a pollution primitive. The Node HTTP adapter is not implicated by this reproduction.

## Observed behavior

Tested against the registry package, using a loopback HTTP server, with both the CommonJS and ESM Axios builds:

| Runtime / Axios | Polluted fetchOptions | Server result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Node 22.19.0 / 1.18.1 | empty, cache, or redirect options | Caller header missing; inherited test Authorization transmitted |
| Node 22.19.0 / 1.20.0 | `{}` | Caller header preserved; no inherited Authorization |
| Node 22.19.0 / 1.20.0 | `{ cache: "no-store" }` or `{ redirect: "follow" }` | Caller header missing; inherited test Authorization transmitted |
| Node 24.15.0 / 1.20.0 | all three options above | Caller header missing; inherited test Authorization transmitted |

All unpolluted baselines preserve the caller header. A second reviewer independently obtained the same results. The expanded assertions exit 1 for the remaining unsafe cases.

## Minimal reproduction

Install `axios@1.20.0`, save this as `repro.mjs`, and run `node repro.mjs` on either runtime:

```js
import axios from "axios";
import http from "node:http";

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.setHeader("content-type", "application/json");
  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));
});
await new Promise(resolve => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
const url = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/`;
let failures = 0;

try {
  for (const polluted of [false, true]) {
    for (const fetchOptions of [{}, { cache: "no-store" }, { redirect: "follow" }]) {
      if (polluted) Object.prototype.headers = {
        Authorization: "Bearer inherited-test-value"
      };
      try {
        const { data } = await axios.get(url, {
          adapter: "fetch",
          headers: { "X-Ordinary": "yes" },
          fetchOptions
        });
        const safe = data["x-ordinary"] === "yes" && !data.authorization;
        console.log({ node: process.version, axios: axios.VERSION,
          polluted, fetchOptions, ordinary: data["x-ordinary"],
          authorization: data.authorization, safe });
        if (!safe) failures++;
      } finally {
        delete Object.prototype.headers;
      }
    }
  }
} finally {
  server.closeAllConnections();
  await new Promise(resolve => server.close(resolve));
}
process.exitCode = failures ? 1 : 0;
```

All requests go to loopback. The Authorization value is a harmless canary, not a credential.

## Expected behavior

Caller-owned resolved headers stay in effect, inherited headers are never sent, and legitimate cache/redirect fetch options remain supported.

## Possible cause

Axios 1.20.0 sanitizes fetch options into a null-prototype object before `fetch(request, safeFetchOptions)`. In Undici, RequestInit conversion creates an ordinary dictionary, and the Request constructor can subsequently read inherited `init.headers`.

On Node 22 the nonempty cache/redirect option activates the headers replacement path. On Node 24 the converted init's default priority appears to activate that path even with empty options. This explanation is an inference from source plus the executed request results, rather than a proposed patch.

References: [Node 22 Request](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.19.0/deps/undici/src/lib/web/fetch/request.js), [Node 24 dictionary conversion](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v24.15.0/deps/undici/src/lib/web/webidl/index.js#L429), [Node 24 Request](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v24.15.0/deps/undici/src/lib/web/fetch/request.js#L442).

## Problem

Calling `error.toJSON()` on an `AxiosError` (e.g. from an error logger such as pino, winston, or Sentry) can cause unbounded memory growth and crash the process with `JavaScript heap out of memory` when the request config includes a Node `http.Agent`/`https.Agent`, even under moderate concurrency.

This is a realistic, common configuration — using a shared `httpAgent`/`httpsAgent` for connection reuse is a widely recommended pattern — so any failed request logged through a standard error logger can trigger it.

## Reproduction

```js
import http from 'node:http';
import https from 'node:https';
import axios from 'axios';

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(500, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ message: 'duplicate key error' }));
});

server.listen(0, async () => {
  const { port } = server.address();
  const instance = axios.create({
    httpAgent: new http.Agent(),
    httpsAgent: new https.Agent(),
    timeout: 5000,
  });

  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: 25 }, () =>
    instance.get(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/`).catch((error) => {
      error.toJSON(); // realistic call site: any error logger
    })
  ));
  server.close();
});
```

Run with:

```
node --max-old-space-size=512 repro.mjs
```

Result:

```
FATAL ERROR: Ineffective mark-compacts near heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory
```

## Environment

- axios 1.2.0 through current (verified against latest `v1.x`)
- Node.js (reproduced on v26.7.0; the Agent/Socket object shape causing this is stable across Node versions)

---

## Early Copilot findings / suggestions

*The following analysis was produced with AI assistance to help maintainers get started faster. It has not been independently verified by a human and should be treated as a starting point, not a conclusion.*

### Suspected root cause

`AxiosError.prototype.toJSON()` calls `utils.toJSONObject(this.config)` unconditionally. `toJSONObject`'s cycle detection is ancestor-path-only (it tracks the current recursion path, not a global "already visited" set). This appears intentional — it preserves correct serialization of shared sibling/DAG references instead of silently dropping them. The tradeoff: any object reachable via multiple distinct paths gets fully re-serialized once per path.

Node's `http(s).Agent` / `Socket` / `TLSSocket` internals appear to have exactly this shape — heavily cross-referenced (agent ↔ sockets ↔ parser ↔ request ↔ response ↔ socket). Serializing `config.httpAgent`/`httpsAgent` therefore walks and re-walks large, overlapping subgraphs, and the cost seems to compound with concurrent in-flight requests sharing one agent.

### Bisect

Bisecting against the repro above points to the origin as commit `b7ee49f6` (PR #5247, "Added toJSONObject util", merged 2022-11-22, first shipped in axios 1.2.0), which changed `AxiosError.prototype.toJSON()` from:

```js
config: this.config
```

to:

```js
config: utils.toJSONObject(this.config)
```

i.e. from a shallow reference to a recursive deep-clone. The repro passes cleanly against the parent commit (`a372b4ce`) and OOMs immediately on `b7ee49f6` itself, with the same script and same `--max-old-space-size` limit.

Also checked: the later refactor in PR #10832 ("use WeakSet for cycle detection in toJSONObject") does **not** appear to be related. That PR only swapped the cycle-detection data structure (`Array` stack → `WeakSet`) while preserving identical ancestor-path-only semantics. The repro crashes equally (same magnitude, same threshold) on commits immediately before and after #10832.

### Possible directions (unverified, for discussion)

- Don't deep-serialize `httpAgent`/`httpsAgent` (and similar platform handles) in `AxiosError.toJSON()` — replace with a shallow marker (e.g. `'[http.Agent]'`) rather than recursing into Node internals.
- Add a depth or node-count budget to `toJSONObject` to bound worst-case work regardless of graph shape.
- Consider excluding known Node core objects (`net.Socket`, `tls.TLSSocket`, `http.Agent`, `http.IncomingMessage`, `http.ClientRequest`) from recursive serialization by default.

## Describe the issue

With the Node http adapter and a keep-alive agent, every idle pooled socket keeps a reference to the last request that used it, and through it that request's full response. This happens with axios's default settings, because requests go through follow-redirects.

`handleRequestSocket` sets `socket[kAxiosCurrentReq] = req`, and the only code that clears it is `req.once('close', clearCurrentReq)` (added in #10800). When `maxRedirects` is not 0, `req` is a follow-redirects `RedirectableRequest`, which never emits `close`: it only re-emits `abort`, `aborted`, `connect`, `error`, `socket` and `timeout` from the native request. So `clearCurrentReq` never runs and the tag stays set until the socket is reused.

This is separate from the leak fixed in #11091 and still reproduces on 1.20.0, which includes that fix. It is bounded at one response per idle socket, but with large pools and large responses it adds up: 600 sockets and 3 MB responses would pin about 1.8 GB.

## Example code

```js
// node --expose-gc repro.mjs
import http from 'node:http';
import axios from 'axios';

const BODY = Buffer.alloc(1024 * 1024, 'x');
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => res.end(BODY));
await new Promise((r) => server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', r));
const url = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/`;

const agent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 20 });
const maxRedirects = process.argv[2] === 'native' ? 0 : undefined;

const refs = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: 20 }, async () => {
    const res = await axios.get(url, { httpAgent: agent, responseType: 'arraybuffer', maxRedirects });
    refs.push(new WeakRef(res.data));
  }));
}
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) { global.gc(); await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50)); }

console.log('responses still alive:', refs.filter((r) => r.deref()).length);
console.log('external MB:', (process.memoryUsage().external / 1e6).toFixed(1));
agent.destroy(); server.close();
```

## Expected behavior

Once a response has been consumed, nothing in axios keeps it alive, so after GC no responses remain, as with `maxRedirects: 0`.

## Actual behavior

| | responses alive after GC (of 200) | external memory |
| --- | --- | --- |
| default (follow-redirects) | 20, one per idle socket | 47.4 MB |
| `maxRedirects: 0` | 0 | 5.5 MB |
| default, `socket[kAxiosCurrentReq]` cleared by hand before GC | 0 | 5.5 MB |

Every idle socket in `agent.freeSockets` still holds a `RedirectableRequest` under the `axios.http.currentReq` symbol.

## Possible fix

Also release the sockets when the final response closes. With this change applied to 1.20.0 (`lib/adapters/http.js`, and the same change in `dist/node/axios.cjs`), the reproduction above reports 0 responses alive in both modes, and a redirect (302 then 200) and a plain request still behave normally. The unit suite (`vitest run --project unit`, Node 26.9.0) gives the same result with and without the change: 1,061 of 1,062 pass. The one failure, `fetch.test.js` "should reject a response whose Content-Length exceeds maxContentLength with ERR_BAD_RESPONSE", also fails on unmodified 1.20.0 on this Node version.

```diff
--- a/lib/adapters/http.js
+++ b/lib/adapters/http.js
@@ -1137,6 +1137,9 @@

       // Create the request
       req = transport.request(options, function handleResponse(res) {
+        // follow-redirects' RedirectableRequest never emits 'close', so also
+        // release the sockets when the final response closes.
+        res.once('close', clearCurrentReq);
         clearConnectPhaseTimer();

         if (req.destroyed) return;
@@ -1370,7 +1373,7 @@
         boundSockets.add(socket);
       });

-      req.once('close', function clearCurrentReq() {
+      function clearCurrentReq() {
         clearConnectPhaseTimer();

         for (const socket of boundSockets) {
@@ -1379,7 +1382,8 @@
           }
         }
         boundSockets.clear();
-      });
+      }
+      req.once('close', clearCurrentReq);

       // Handle request timeout
       if (own('timeout')) {
```

`clearCurrentReq` is safe to call twice: the second call finds `boundSockets` empty.

## Environment

- Axios version: 1.20.0 (follow-redirects 1.16.1)
- Adapter: http
- Runtime: Node 26.9.0
- OS: macOS
- Additional context: keep-alive `http.Agent`; the leak needs `maxRedirects` left at its default.

This came out of testing AI coding models on real bugs: Claude Opus flagged the uncleared tag while diagnosing the leak fixed in #11091. The reproduction, the candidate fix and the test runs above were done with Claude Code.

## Describe the issue

In production we see an uncaught `TypeError: args is not iterable (cannot read property null)` thrown from the trailing timer in `lib/helpers/throttle.js`, shortly after a successful XHR upload with `onUploadProgress` set. The request itself succeeds; the error is thrown from a detached `setTimeout` callback afterwards.

The timer callback calls `invoke(lastArgs)` without checking `lastArgs`:

```js
timer = setTimeout(() => {
  timer = null;
  invoke(lastArgs);   // lastArgs can be null here
}, threshold - passed);
```

`invoke` (and therefore `flush`) sets `lastArgs = null` and clears the timer. Normally this means the timer can never fire with `lastArgs === null`. However, in every one of our error events the page's `window.setTimeout` had already been replaced by third-party code (likely a browser extension or security product injecting into the page) before our app loaded. A wrapper that does not return the real timer id breaks the `if (!timer)` / `clearTimeout(timer)` bookkeeping: the throttle schedules several timers it can never cancel, and after `flush()` the leftovers fire with `lastArgs === null`.

Before #6938 ("use spread operator instead of '.apply()'") this case was a silent no-op (`fn.apply(null, null)`); since then `fn(...null)` throws. `flush` already guards this state (`lastArgs && invoke(lastArgs)`); the timer path does not.

Suggested fix (one line, consistent with `flush`):

```js
timer = setTimeout(() => {
  timer = null;
  lastArgs && invoke(lastArgs);
}, threshold - passed);
```

Skipping is lossless: `lastArgs === null` means the latest event was already delivered.

## Example code

Uses the unmodified `lib/helpers/throttle.js` (verified on 1.18.1 and 1.20.0):

```js
import throttle from 'axios/lib/helpers/throttle.js';

// Simulate a third-party setTimeout wrapper that drops the return value.
const realSetTimeout = globalThis.setTimeout;
globalThis.setTimeout = function (...args) { realSetTimeout.apply(this, args); };

const [throttled, flush] = throttle(() => {}, 3);
throttled({ loaded: 1 });
throttled({ loaded: 2 }); // schedules trailing timer (id lost)
throttled({ loaded: 3 }); // schedules another one
flush();                  // delivers latest event, cannot cancel the timers
// ~330 ms later: TypeError: args is not iterable (cannot read property null)
```

With the native `setTimeout` the same sequence does not throw.

## Expected behavior

A trailing timer that fires when there is no pending event does nothing, like `flush()`.

## Actual behavior

Uncaught `TypeError: args is not iterable (cannot read property null)` (V8 wording; minified as `c is not iterable`) from the throttle's timer callback, after a successful upload.

## Environment

- Axios version: 1.18.1 in production; `throttle.js` unchanged in 1.20.0 and on `v1.x`
- Adapter: xhr
- Runtime: Chrome / Edge 153–155 on Windows
- OS: Windows 10/11
- Additional context: Vue 3 / Nuxt app, Sentry browser SDK. Sentry's `BrowserApiErrors` integration reports the wrapped `setTimeout` as `<anonymous>` in all events (a clean browser reports `setTimeout`), which is how we determined that `setTimeout` was replaced before the app loaded. We could not identify the specific extension or product doing the wrapping.

## Describe the issue

The Node HTTP adapter fails to decode a `Content-Encoding: deflate` response when the body is a valid zlib stream written with a window smaller than 32K. `ZlibHeaderTransformStream` only treats a body whose first byte is `0x78` as already zlib-wrapped. RFC 1950 allows CMF bytes `0x08` to `0x78` (CINFO 0 to 7), and zlib writes `0x18` to `0x68` for `windowBits` 9 to 14. Those bodies get a second `78 9C` header prepended, and inflate then fails.

## Example code

```js
import http from 'node:http';
import zlib from 'node:zlib';
import axios from 'axios';

const body = zlib.deflateSync('hello', { windowBits: 9 }); // starts with 0x18 0x95

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Encoding', 'deflate');
  res.end(body);
}).listen(0, async () => {
  try {
    const { data } = await axios.get(`http://localhost:${server.address().port}`);
    console.log(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(err.code, err.message);
  } finally {
    server.close();
  }
});
```

## Expected behavior

`hello` is printed, as with `zlib.inflateSync(body)` or `curl --compressed`.

## Actual behavior

The request rejects with `Z_DATA_ERROR` (`invalid stored block lengths`).

## Environment

- Axios version: v1.x at 326843a (1.20.0)
- Adapter: http
- Runtime: Node 22.23
- OS: Linux
- Additional context: none

